    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The recent collapses of First Republic Bank, Silicon Valley Bank and Signature Bank have reminded the financial world of the devastating impacts of bank failures. Economists have long sought to predict such failures to prevent their consequences and thus far, their empirical predictive models have predominantly evolved by taking advantage of greater computing power, availability of data and sophisticated computational techniques. Sophisticated as these models are, their inputs have largely gone unchanged over time, focusing on financial ratios and risk measures to depict a bank’s liquidity, solvency, and general capital adequacy. Behavioral Economics and recent history, however, illustrate that bank failures occur not just because of a bank’s financial position but also because of its depositors’ behavior. Therefore, I ask, can using deposit statistics improve the predictive performance of models that predict bank failure? I use the financial data of failed and surviving U.S. banks from 2007 to 2017, a decade of several bank failures. I find that models built off of deposit data are less effective than models built on capitalization when predicting large banks’ failure but more effective than models built on capitalization when predicting intermediate banks’ failure and when predicting small banks’ failure, with deposit data becoming more important as banks’ sizes decrease.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Jankelow, J. (2024). BACK TO THE WITHDRAWING BOARD: USING DEPOSIT DATA TO PREDICT U.S. BANK FAILURES. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/jg077qq2636. https://doi.org/10.25740/jg077qq2636.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
